{
  "topic": "category theory",
  "slug": "category-theory",
  "created": "2026-08-20",
  "student_level": "intermediate",
  "prerequisites": [
    "Categories: objects, morphisms, composition, identity, isomorphism",
    "Functors: covariant and contravariant, preservation of composition and identity",
    "Natural transformations: components, naturality square, vertical and horizontal composition",
    "Basic abstract algebra: groups, rings, modules, homomorphisms",
    "Elementary set theory: functions, injections, surjections, bijections",
    "Familiarity with topological spaces (helpful but not strictly required)"
  ],
  "exit_criteria": [
    "Recognize universal properties in concrete mathematical constructions and verify them formally via uniqueness of mediating morphisms",
    "State and apply the Yoneda lemma to prove isomorphisms, characterize representable functors, and derive the co-Yoneda lemma",
    "Identify adjunctions across algebra, topology, order theory, and programming, and verify them via hom-set bijection or unit-counit triangle identities",
    "Recognize when RAPL/LAPC constrains adjoint existence, and use limit-preservation arguments to determine whether a functor can have an adjoint",
    "Construct Eilenberg-Moore and Kleisli categories for a monad, and explain how monads encode algebraic theories and model computational effects via Moggi's framework",
    "Read string-diagrammatic proofs in monoidal categories and translate between enriched-categorical language and concrete structures such as metric spaces and ordered sets",
    "Connect Lawvere's enriched framework to Wasserstein distance from optimal transport, and trace how the Giry monad bridges probability, measure theory, and categorical algebra",
    "Read category-theoretic arguments in research papers and textbooks without constant reference lookups, and articulate which advanced direction -- topos theory, higher category theory, or applied category theory -- to pursue next"
  ],
